.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt OPENAT2 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm openat2
.Nd open a file, with limits on how its path is resolved
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In linux/openat2.h
.Ft long
.Fn syscall SYS_openat2 "int dirfd" "const char *path" "struct open_how *how" "size_t size"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn openat2
opens
.Fa path
as
.Xr openat 2
does, with the flags and mode in
.Fa how ,
which is
.Fa size
bytes long, and with the limits its
.Va resolve
member sets on the walk of the path.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct open_how {
    uint64_t flags;   /* O_ flags, as openat(2) takes them */
    uint64_t mode;    /* the new file's mode, with O_CREAT or O_TMPFILE */
    uint64_t resolve; /* RESOLVE_ flags */
};
.Ed
.Pp
The structure is 24 bytes and is the same for i386 and x86-64.
A larger
.Fa size
is accepted, up to a page, if the bytes past the 24 are zero, so that a
program built for a later version of the structure runs.
.Pp
Unlike
.Xr openat 2 ,
.Fn openat2
refuses a flag it does not know, a mode without
.Dv O_CREAT
or
.Dv O_TMPFILE ,
and a mode bit above 07777.
.Pp
The
.Va resolve
flags are:
.Bl -tag -width RESOLVE_NO_MAGICLINKS
.It Dv RESOLVE_NO_XDEV
Every name of the path is on the mount the walk starts on: the walk never
crosses onto another mount, downward, by
.Ql ..
or by a link.
.It Dv RESOLVE_NO_MAGICLINKS
The walk follows no link of
.Pa /proc
that refers to an object instead of to a path: a descriptor's
.Pa /proc/PID/fd/N ,
a process's
.Pa cwd ,
.Pa root
and
.Pa exe ,
and a namespace's link.
.It Dv RESOLVE_NO_SYMLINKS
The walk follows no link at all, those of
.Pa /proc
among them.
.It Dv RESOLVE_BENEATH
The walk stays under
.Fa dirfd :
an absolute path, an absolute link,
.Ql ..
at
.Fa dirfd
and a link of
.Pa /proc
to an object are refused.
.It Dv RESOLVE_IN_ROOT
The walk treats
.Fa dirfd
as its root, as
.Xr chroot 2
would: an absolute path and an absolute link start there, and
.Ql ..
there stays there.
A link of
.Pa /proc
to an object is refused.
.It Dv RESOLVE_CACHED
The walk takes only names the kernel's name cache already holds, and
fails with
.Er EAGAIN
where it would read a directory, so that a caller can try a fast open
first and do the slow one elsewhere.
.El
.Pp
A rename or a mount anywhere in the system while a walk under
.Dv RESOLVE_BENEATH
or
.Dv RESOLVE_IN_ROOT
takes
.Ql ..
may have moved the walk out of
.Fa dirfd ;
the call then fails with
.Er EAGAIN
and the caller tries again.
.Ss System call numbers
The call is 437 on both i386 and x86-64.
The x86-64 kernel adds
.Dv O_LARGEFILE
to every open but an
.Dv O_PATH
one, for an i386 program too, as Linux's 64-bit kernel does.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The call returns the new descriptor, or \-1 with
.Va errno
set.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/openat2probe.c
checks the size rules and the strict checks of the flags and the mode,
and each
.Va resolve
flag on a tree of directories and links in tmpfs: a link and
.Ql ..
out of the directory under
.Dv RESOLVE_BENEATH
and
.Dv RESOLVE_IN_ROOT ,
a link through
.Pa /proc/self/fd ,
a crossing onto
.Pa /proc ,
and
.Dv RESOLVE_CACHED
for a name that is cached and for an open that would create.
.Sh ERRORS
.Fn openat2
fails with the errors of
.Xr openat 2
and these:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er E2BIG
.Fa size
is larger than a page, or a byte of
.Fa how
past the 24 it knows is not zero.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa size
is smaller than 24; a flag or a
.Va resolve
flag is unknown; both
.Dv RESOLVE_BENEATH
and
.Dv RESOLVE_IN_ROOT
are set; the mode is not zero without
.Dv O_CREAT
or
.Dv O_TMPFILE ,
or has a bit above 07777;
.Dv O_CREAT
is set with
.Dv O_DIRECTORY ;
or
.Dv O_PATH
is set with a flag but
.Dv O_DIRECTORY ,
.Dv O_NOFOLLOW
and
.Dv O_CLOEXEC .
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
.Dv RESOLVE_CACHED
is set and a name is not in the cache, or the flags would create or
truncate; or a rename or a mount raced a walk under
.Dv RESOLVE_BENEATH
or
.Dv RESOLVE_IN_ROOT .
.It Bq Er ELOOP
.Dv RESOLVE_NO_SYMLINKS
is set and the walk meets a link it would follow, or
.Dv RESOLVE_NO_MAGICLINKS
is set and it meets a link of
.Pa /proc
to an object.
.It Bq Er EXDEV
.Dv RESOLVE_BENEATH
is set and the walk would leave
.Fa dirfd ;
.Dv RESOLVE_BENEATH
or
.Dv RESOLVE_IN_ROOT
is set and the walk meets a link of
.Pa /proc
to an object; or
.Dv RESOLVE_NO_XDEV
is set and the walk would cross onto another mount.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Fa how
or
.Fa path
is outside the caller's memory.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chroot 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr openat 2
.Sh STANDARDS
The call is Linux's, and its rules are those of Linux 6.12.
musl does not wrap it; a program calls it through
.Xr syscall 2 .
.Pp
The kernel checks a walk under a
.Va resolve
flag first, and then opens the path that walk reached, which has no
link and no
.Ql ..
left in it; if a rename or a mount changes the tree between the two, it
walks again, four times in all, before it fails with
.Er EAGAIN
even for a flag Linux gives that error only under
.Dv RESOLVE_BENEATH
and
.Dv RESOLVE_IN_ROOT .
A link whose target climbs with
.Ql ..
from a directory of
.Pa /proc ,
.Pa /dev
or
.Pa /sys
under
.Dv RESOLVE_BENEATH
or
.Dv RESOLVE_IN_ROOT
fails with
.Er EXDEV ,
where Linux may follow it.
